# 5. Analyze Authentication Failures

Search for failed logins:

```bash
grep "Failed password" auth-events.log
```

Count them:

```bash
grep -c "Failed password" auth-events.log
```

Search successful logins:

```bash
grep "Accepted" auth-events.log
```

Search sudo activity:

```bash
grep "sudo" auth-events.log
```

---

# 6. Analyze Source IPs

Extract source IPs:

```bash
grep "Failed password" auth-events.log | awk '{print $(NF-3)}'
```

Count occurrences:

```bash
grep "Failed password" auth-events.log | awk '{print $(NF-3)}' | sort | uniq -c
```

You should see the concept:

```text
5 192.168.1.50
2 192.168.1.20
```

This tells us that:

```text
192.168.1.50 → 5 failures
192.168.1.20 → 2 failures
```

---

# 7. Correlate Failed and Successful Logins

Search:

```bash
grep -E "Failed password|Accepted" auth-events.log
```

You should notice:

```text
192.168.1.50
    ↓
5 failed attempts
    ↓
successful login
```

This is more interesting than simply seeing five failed logins.

However:

> A successful login after failed attempts does not automatically prove account compromise.

Additional evidence is required.

---

# 8. Investigate Privileged Activity

Search for sudo:

```bash
grep "sudo" auth-events.log
```

Example:

```text
Oct 02 10:04:12 server sudo: admin : COMMAND=/usr/bin/systemctl status ssh
```

Record:

```text
User:
admin

Command:
systemctl status ssh

Time:
10:04:12
```

Now correlate:

```text
Failed SSH attempts
        ↓
Successful login
        ↓
sudo command
```

